    "content": "<p>In November 2025, public procurement teams in Sweden were dealing with a live issue: <strong>roadmap</strong> colliding with day-to-day tender work. This was not an abstract policy debate — it showed up in deadlines, clarification logs and supplier conversations.</p>\n<h2>AI in practice</h2>\n<p>Buyers needed clearer structure for AI. Spreadsheets and private inboxes created uneven information and weak audit trails. Suppliers felt the same friction when status was unclear or documents lived in multiple places.</p>\n<h2>What DIS meant for systems</h2>\n<p>Platforms that supported DIS reduced rework. Searchable supplier data, public Q&amp;A, and guided workflows helped teams stay compliant while moving faster. Where those capabilities were missing, organisations paid in overtime and failed deliveries.</p>\n<h2>Looking forward from November</h2>\n<p>The organisations that treated data as a product requirement — not a side note — built habits that still matter. Digital e-procurement is not about publishing a notice; it is about running a trustworthy process from market research to contract.</p>\n<p><em>Written for practitioners navigating public procurement in November 2025.</em></p>",
    "titleSv": "Förberedelser av plattformsroadmaps för 2026",
